# Helpers and scalar input controls

A numeric field can edit a household preference or command physical equipment. Establish which one it is before choosing the control and feedback. A helper's saved value may later influence an automation; “local helper” does not mean “has no consequence”.

<h2 id="values">Value families and their meaning</h2>

| Native value or helper | Contract |
| --- | --- |
| Number / input number | Preserve min, max, step, unit and the supported slider/box presentation. Explain whether saving changes a device setting or a helper. |
| Select / input select | Show only the supplied options. Preserve the stored option exactly, including case and translated/custom labels. |
| Text / input text | Respect length and text/password mode. Keep a visible label; never expose a password in a summary or status message. |
| Date, datetime, time / input datetime | Preserve which parts exist, local/time-zone semantics and validity. Do not add a time to a date-only value. |
| Switch / input boolean | Name the actual enabled setting or requested power change. Do not label every boolean “On” without its subject. |
| Counter | Preserve current value, configured step/bounds and separate increment, decrement and reset operations. Reset is not always zero. |
| Timer / schedule | Preserve the native active, paused and idle states or schedule intervals. Use the dedicated [time contract](schedules-and-time.html). |

A group summarises members; it is not automatically a new physical device. A scene applies stored settings; it does not prove that all members now match. A script runs a sequence; its execution state is not confirmation of every resulting physical action. Automation enabled state controls future runs, not the truth of its conditions. Keep these distinctions when a helper appears in an Entities row, Tile feature or More info dialog.

