<h2 id="editing">Draft, saved value and observed result</h2>

Keep three states separate: the draft being edited, the value accepted by Home Assistant, and a device's later report where a device is involved. Do not replace an in-progress draft when a subscription update arrives; show the conflict and let the person resolve it. Validate using the current schema before dispatch and preserve input after rejection. If a selected option disappears, retain an unresolved-value explanation instead of selecting the first replacement.

Apply pending feedback to the affected control, not the entire household page. Prevent duplicate equivalent requests while the outcome is pending. A network timeout leaves the result unconfirmed; do not resend automatically. Local documentation forms below check draft validity only and say so explicitly.

<h2 id="accessibility">Accessible scalar controls</h2>

Use a labelled text input, select, checkbox, radios, number field or native range according to the actual value. A read-only number is text, not a disabled slider. Keep current and draft values distinguishable. Buttons that increase or decrease need target and step context. Do not intercept arrow keys outside the focused control, and avoid wheel changes while the person scrolls the page.

Locale formatting must not change the serialised value. Native date/time inputs need clear timezone context where an instant is involved. Colour choices need meaningful named alternatives and accessible values; a colour swatch alone does not identify a mode or state.

<h2 id="acceptance">Acceptance checks</h2>

Test decimal/negative bounds, a non-zero counter reset, missing choice, hidden password, date-only value and daylight-saving ambiguity. Try a repeated request and an external update while editing. Verify that opening a helper or editing an unsaved draft sends no additional action. Creating or saving a helper can change native state and trigger dependent automations; preserve and explain those consequences. Confirm unsupported remote/siren actions are absent and that command acceptance is not presented as receiver confirmation.

Sources: [Number](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/number/), [Select](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/select/), [Input text](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/input_text/), [Input datetime](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/input_datetime/), [Counter](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/counter/), [Button](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/button/) and [Remote](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/remote/).

<h2 id="evidence">Implementation and evidence boundary</h2>

This is a reference design specification, not a replacement for native Home Assistant components. The examples use invented records and local draft feedback; they do not call Home Assistant. Mapping a registry entry here establishes a design owner, not proof that every integration, browser or device has been tested. Keep native state formatting, translations, permissions, capabilities and event handling. Theme tokens can change presentation; they cannot supply missing application behaviour.

The [interface coverage inventory](../reference/ha-interface-coverage.json) records the finite source baseline and exact mappings. Recheck native behaviour after frontend changes. Use the [native task acceptance process](native-task-accessibility.html) to record what was actually tested.

Apply the [design styles](../styles/index.html), [writing guide](../content/index.html), [layout](../styles/layout.html), [colour roles](../styles/colour.html) and [typography](../styles/typography.html). Structural dividers use the quiet border token; editable boundaries and focus indicators remain distinct. Preserve 16px native control text, visible labels, keyboard access and readable names at 320px. A compact arrangement must not remove a capability explanation or essential recovery route.

